\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Temporal Abstraction.}
The options framework \citep{sutton1999options} and model-free action-repetition methods \citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl, lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} achieve temporal abstraction by training values on real transitions, insulating them from model-induced bias. In world-model RL, the Dreamer family \citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3} and transformer-based variants \citep{micheli2023iris, robine2023twm, hafner2025dreamerv4} train policies in imagination at fixed $\dt{=}1$. Several works introduce temporal abstraction via hierarchical representations \citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros}, goal-conditioned jumps \citep{li2025lsimagine}, or multi-scale state-space models \citep{shaj2023mts3}, but none conditions a flat dynamics model on $\dt$ for imagination-based policy training.

\paragraph{TAWM and the MPC--Imagination Contrast.}
TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains. The critical distinction is architectural: TAWM re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into the critic. We show that identical $\dt$-conditioning fails in DreamerV3's imagination loop, where errors compound through bootstrapping---transforming manageable imprecision into pathological value overestimation.

\paragraph{Value Overestimation.}
Value overestimation is well-studied in model-free \citep{fujimoto2018td3, vanhasselt2016double} and model-based settings \citep{janner2019mbpo, yu2020mopo}. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints; our CIVO is mechanistically distinct, arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. \citet{tallec2019making} show Q-function degradation with temporal resolution mismatches, underpinning our finding at $\dt > 1$. \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts} demonstrate rollout-quality dominance in MBRL; we identify the specific $\dt$-embedding-induced reward bias by which rollout divergence corrupts values. Our factorial diagnostic approach follows \citet{engstrom2020implementation} and \citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters}.

% --- APPENDIX: NVE Threshold Sensitivity Analysis ---

\appendix
\section{NVE Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_sensitivity}

We report the full sensitivity analysis for the NVE diagnostic framework introduced in Section~\ref{sec:results}. The main text reports Task~B2 (any CIVO detection, $\nve \geq 1.5\times$). Here we additionally present Task~B1, which restricts the positive class to \emph{sustained} CIVO---experiments exhibiting persistent, accelerating value overestimation that progresses to performance collapse. Transient CIVO cases (e.g., exp~009b, which self-corrects after a brief excursion above the threshold) are classified as negative in B1.

\subsection{Per-Experiment NVE Values}

Table~\ref{tab:nve_raw} lists the NVE values at 35K+ training steps for all eight factorial experiments. Experiments are sorted by descending NVE. Experiment 008e\_v3\_s2 exhibits elevated NVE ($3.78\times$) due to Imagination-Reality Divergence (IRD), a mechanistically distinct phenomenon from CIVO, and is classified as negative in both tasks.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-experiment NVE values (35K+ steps) and ground truth labels. $+$ = positive (CIVO), $-$ = negative. IRD = Imagination-Reality Divergence; trans.\ = transient CIVO with self-correction.}
\label{tab:nve_raw}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}
\toprule
Experiment & NVE & Mechanism & B1 & B2 \\
\midrule
006          & 4.84 & sustained CIVO & $+$ & $+$ \\
008d         & 4.13 & sustained CIVO & $+$ & $+$ \\
008e\_v3\_s2 & 3.78 & IRD (non-CIVO) & $-$ & $-$ \\
009b         & 1.62 & transient CIVO & $-$ & $+$ \\
001          & 1.00 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\
008f         & 0.97 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\
008e\_v3\_s1 & 0.94 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\
008a         & 0.93 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Multi-Threshold Classification Performance}

Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1} reports Task~B1 classification metrics across six NVE thresholds. Task~B1 achieves $\text{AUROC} = 1.000$ because the minimum positive NVE ($4.13\times$, exp~008d) exceeds the maximum negative NVE ($3.78\times$, exp~008e\_v3\_s2), yielding perfect rank separation. The optimal threshold lies in the interval $(3.78, 4.13]$; we report $\geq 3.9\times$ as a representative value. Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2} provides the corresponding analysis for Task~B2 ($\text{AUROC} = 0.933$, imperfect separation due to 008e\_v3\_s2).

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Task~B1}: Sustained CIVO detection sensitivity analysis ($n_{\text{pos}}=2$, $n_{\text{neg}}=6$, $n=8$, $\text{AUROC}=1.000$). All confidence intervals are two-sided Clopper-Pearson exact binomial ($\alpha=0.05$). Thresholds 1.2$\times$/1.5$\times$ and 1.7$\times$/2.0$\times$ yield identical operating points (no NVE values in those intervals). $^\dagger$Optimal threshold in $(3.78, 4.13]$.}
\label{tab:sensitivity_b1}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}
\toprule
