\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, regardless of mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline (exp\_001) reaches 915.7 at 105K steps and peaks at 970.4 at 505K. In contrast, fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) peaks at 485.3 at 35K before collapsing to 132.9 by 65K---a $-73\%$ decline from its own peak (14\% of baseline). Fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at only 176.9 at 25K and never exceeds 152.5 thereafter. The learned-gate configuration (exp\_005) peaks at 541.2 at 55K but collapses to 98.8 by 145K. This uniform degradation motivates the factorial analysis that follows.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves for all configurations. The $\dt{=}1$ baseline (black) reaches 915.7 at 105K; all variable-rate configurations degrade, with fixed $\dt{=}4$ and the learned gate collapsing most severely.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the factor that reliably triggers pathological value overestimation. The key causal control compares exp\_008d (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) against exp\_008f (identical configuration but \emph{no} trained $\dt$-embedding). As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d} (trained): $\nve = 1.78\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $4.12\times$ at 55K $\rightarrow$ collapse (eval 167$\rightarrow$82.8).
\item \textbf{008f} (no dt\_emb): $\nve = 1.02\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $0.30\times$ at 105K $\rightarrow$ no collapse (eval 503.0 at 105K).
\end{itemize}

In the 35K+ window (\S\ref{sec:metrics}), the $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold correctly classifies 7/8 seeds (AUROC${=}0.933$, Clopper--Pearson 95\% CI $[0.47, 1.0]$): 3 CIVO-positive (exp\_006, 008d, 009b) and 4 CIVO-negative, with one non-CIVO false positive (008e\_v3 s2, $3.78\times$; \S\ref{sec:f6}).  The gap between the lowest true positive ($1.62\times$) and the highest true negative ($1.00\times$) is $0.62$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$) trajectories. The dashed line marks the $1.5\times$ threshold. All trained-embedding configurations with $\dt > 1$ imagination (red/orange) exceed this threshold; the mitigation configuration (008e\_v3 s1, dash-dot) with $\dt{=}1$-only imagination remains below it.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Three post-CIVO outcomes.}
Running the 008d configuration with two independent seeds (s1, s2) reveals that trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO---yet the downstream consequence is stochastic. Exp\_009e extends seed~1 (008d) beyond 55K to 105K steps:

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 3: Three post-CIVO outcomes
%% ============================================================

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Post-CIVO outcomes for the 008d configuration. Peak/Final NVE measured in the 35K+ window. Seeds 1 and 2 are independent runs; 009e extends seed~1 (008d) from 55K to 105K.}
\label{tab:post_civo}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Peak} & \textbf{Final} & \textbf{Eval} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
s1 (008d) & 4.12$\times$ & 4.12$\times$ & 82.8 & Full collapse \\
s2 (009b) & 1.62$\times$ & 0.38$\times$ & 267.9 & Transient CIVO \\
s1 ext (009e) & 4.12$\times$ & 1.57$\times$ & 131.1 & Oscillatory \\
